<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Su Nuraxi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Su Nuraxi di Barumini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Su Nuraxi is a Nuragic archaeological site at Barumini in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, whose name means simply &quot;the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuraghe">nuraghe</a>&quot; in Campidanese Sardinian. Its oldest part is a central tower of basalt blocks with three chambers one above another, 18.6 metres high, built between the 17th and 13th centuries BCE; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> four corner towers joined by a wall were built around it, and a village inhabited from the 13th to the 6th century BCE grew up around the whole. First explored by the archaeologist Giovanni Lilliu, its discoverer, it is considered the most impressive expression of the Nuragic civilisation and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/su-nuraxi.jpg" alt="Su Nuraxi" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Norbert Nagel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuraghe_Su_Nuraxi_-_Barumini_-_Sardinia_-_Italy_-_30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>More than 7,000 nuraghi still dot Sardinia, among them <b>Su Nuraxi</b> at Barumini, Santu Antine at Torralba, Nuraghe Losa and Arrubiu at Orroli, and some sanctuaries were reused as Christian churches, such as San Salvatore in Sinis at Cabras.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
